cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below. A
  manuscript describing this research program is in preparation and not yet
  published; this entry will be updated with a DOI/venue once available.
type: software
title: RACA-Collective
abstract: >-
  From Reasoning Allocation to Behavioral Specialization: boundary results
  in multi-robot systems. Studies whether a fleet can learn when reasoning
  is worth its cost, delegate reasoning to peers, and develop behavioral
  specialization without predefined roles. Paper 3 in a research arc that
  started with warehouse-amr-emergent-agents (Paper 1) and RACA (Paper 2).
authors:
  - family-names: "Mansournia"
    given-names: "Pouya"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-1515-6550"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-15"
keywords:
  - multi-robot systems
  - value of computation
  - outcome-aware routing
  - cognitive delegation
  - behavioral specialization
  - emergent collective cognition
